body:
🟡 After a successful `SetVaultSetting`/`DeleteVaultSetting` write in `handleVaultSettingsPatch` (handle_vaults.go:559-563), the handler unconditionally re-reads the policy and returns 500 if that read fails — even though the write committed. The frontend at `SettingsTab.tsx:75-79` rolls back its optimistic update on any non-OK response, leaving the DB holding the new value while the UI shows the old one (a desynced state for an enforcement-relevant security control until the next page load). Trivial fix: echo back the validated input value on the write path and only re-read for the no-op PATCH case.

<details>
<summary>Extended reasoning...</summary>

## What the bug is

`handleVaultSettingsPatch` performs a write (either `SetVaultSetting` or `DeleteVaultSetting`) when `body.UnmatchedHostPolicy != nil`, then unconditionally calls `readUnmatchedHostPolicy` and returns 500 `Failed to read vault settings` if that read errors:

```go
if body.UnmatchedHostPolicy != nil {
    // ... SetVaultSetting or DeleteVaultSetting succeeds ...
}
policy, err := readUnmatchedHostPolicy(ctx, s.store, ns.ID)
if err != nil {
    jsonError(w, http.StatusInternalServerError, "Failed to read vault settings")
    return
}
```

The frontend `handlePolicyToggle` (web/src/pages/vault/SettingsTab.tsx:75-79) treats any `!resp.ok` as "write failed", rolls back the optimistic UI state via `setPolicy(previous)`, and surfaces `Failed to update policy` — even though the DB already holds the new value.

## Step-by-step proof

1. Operator toggles **Strict deny mode** ON in the UI. Frontend optimistically sets `policy = "deny"` and PATCHes `{"unmatched_host_policy":"deny"}`.
2. Server validates the value (line 548), then `SetVaultSetting(ctx, ns.ID, "unmatched_host_policy", "deny")` succeeds — the row is committed.
3. The follow-up `readUnmatchedHostPolicy` errors (e.g. context cancellation between two consecutive in-handler calls, disk pressure, or another transient I/O fault).
4. Handler writes 500 `Failed to read vault settings`.
5. Frontend hits the `!resp.ok` branch, runs `setPolicy(previous)` reverting the UI to `passthrough`, and shows "Failed to update policy".
6. **Desynced state**: the broker now denies unmatched hosts (DB has `deny`), but the operator sees `passthrough` in the UI and was told the toggle failed. Resolves only on next page load.

## Why existing code doesn't prevent it

`readUnmatchedHostPolicy` does fold `sql.ErrNoRows` to `PolicyPassthrough, nil` (so a `DeleteVaultSetting` followed by `Get` returns cleanly), but other `*sql.DB` failures — context cancellation, write-then-disk-removal, etc. — propagate as non-nil errors and trip the 500 path.

## Addressing the refutation

The refuting verifier is right that the trigger is rare under `SetMaxOpenConns(1) + busy_timeout(5000)`: the connection that just succeeded a write is alive, so the next read on the same conn rarely fails transiently. Realistic triggers are narrow — context cancellation between the two in-handler calls, or catastrophic conditions. That's why this is filed as **nit**, not normal — multiple confirming verifiers reached the same probability assessment.

The refutation's stronger claim — that returning 500 is a "defensive" property worth preserving — does not hold. The handler has already validated the input on line 548 (rejecting anything other than `passthrough`/`deny`), and the write either succeeded or returned 500 earlier. There is no scenario in which echoing the validated request value misrepresents server state, because either (a) the write succeeded and the DB now holds that exact value, or (b) the write failed and the handler already returned 500 from the inner branch. The post-write read adds no integrity guarantee — it only converts "this is what was just written" into "this is what we just observed," at the cost of a real desync window for the UI.

## Impact

UI/server divergence on a security-relevant policy toggle. The operator believes "deny" failed to apply when it did (or vice versa), and may either (a) assume the vault is still permissive when it has been locked down, or (b) assume the vault is locked down when it is still permissive. Self-heals on the next `GET /v1/vaults/{name}/settings`, so practical impact is bounded — but the response semantics ("this is the current policy") diverge from actual server state.

## Fix

Echo the validated input on the write path; only re-read on the no-op PATCH:

```go
if body.UnmatchedHostPolicy != nil {
    val := strings.TrimSpace(*body.UnmatchedHostPolicy)
    var effective brokercore.UnmatchedHostPolicy
    if val == "" {
        if err := s.store.DeleteVaultSetting(ctx, ns.ID, settingUnmatchedHostPolicy); err != nil {
            jsonError(w, http.StatusInternalServerError, "Failed to update vault settings")
            return
        }
        effective = brokercore.PolicyPassthrough
    } else {
        policy := brokercore.UnmatchedHostPolicy(val)
        if !brokercore.IsValidUnmatchedHostPolicy(policy) { /* ... */ }
        if err := s.store.SetVaultSetting(ctx, ns.ID, settingUnmatchedHostPolicy, string(policy)); err != nil { /* ... */ }
        effective = policy
    }
    jsonOK(w, map[string]interface{}{"unmatched_host_policy": string(effective)})
    return
}
// No-op PATCH: read current value to return.
policy, err := readUnmatchedHostPolicy(ctx, s.store, ns.ID)
// ...
```

