body:
🔴 The role-change menu items at AllAgentsTab.tsx:67-81 await `apiFetch` but never check `resp.ok` before calling `onDone()`, so 409 (last-owner guard) and 400 (invalid role) responses silently refresh the dropdown with no feedback to the operator. The sibling `AllUsersTab.tsx` `setRoleTo` (also touched by this PR) correctly surfaces errors via `onError`. The PR widens the failure surface here — previously a single `owner↔member` toggle, now up to two destinations per row, plus a brand-new 409 path from the extended `guardLastOwner` (`handle_agents.go:1168`) that fires when demoting the last owner-agent to **any** non-owner role (including `no-access`). Mirror the `AllUsersTab` pattern: read `resp.ok`, parse the error JSON, surface via `setError`.

<details>
<summary>Extended reasoning...</summary>

## What's wrong

`apiFetch` (`web/src/lib/api.ts:13-24`) is a thin wrapper around `fetch()` that returns the `Response` and **does not throw on non-2xx**. The role-change items in `AllAgentsTab.tsx` at lines 67-81 do:

```tsx
onClick: async () => {
  await apiFetch(
    `/v1/agents/${encodeURIComponent(agent.name)}/role`,
    { method: "POST", body: JSON.stringify({ role: opt.role }) }
  );
  onDone();
},
```

There is no `resp.ok` check; `onDone()` runs unconditionally, refetches the agent list, and closes the dropdown. The operator sees no error and the role appears unchanged.

Compare the sibling code path that this PR also modified at `AllUsersTab.tsx:57-69`:

```tsx
async function setRoleTo(newRole: InstanceRole) {
  const resp = await apiFetch(`/v1/admin/users/${encodeURIComponent(user.email)}/role`, {
    method: "POST",
    body: JSON.stringify({ role: newRole }),
  });
  if (!resp.ok) {
    const data = await resp.json().catch(() => ({}));
    onError(data.error || "Failed to change role");
    return;
  }
  onDone();
}
```

That one parses the error and surfaces it via `onError`. The two role columns sit side by side and behave inconsistently after this PR.

## Why this PR enlarges the bug

The previous code used a single owner↔member toggle (1 destination per row). The PR replaces that with iteration over `INSTANCE_ROLE_OPTIONS` (`shared.tsx`), so now there are **up to 2 destinations per agent** — e.g. an owner-agent gets both `Set role: Member` and `Set role: No Access`.

The PR also extends `guardLastOwner` in `internal/server/handle_agents.go`:

```go
// Safety: cannot demote the last owner (user or agent) — to any non-owner role.
if agent.Role == "owner" && body.Role != "owner" && s.guardLastOwner(ctx, w, "demote") {
  return
}
```

The old condition was `body.Role == "member"`. The new condition is `body.Role != "owner"`, which means demoting the last owner-agent to `no-access` now also returns **409 Conflict** — a brand-new failure path that this onClick will swallow silently. The guard fires for **either** new destination on a sole owner-agent.

400 is also reachable: if a future client sends an unknown role, `handleAgentSetRole` returns 400 with `Role must be one of: owner, member, no-access`. Same silent swallow.

## Step-by-step proof

1. Instance has exactly one owner-actor, an agent named `bot`. `bot.role == "owner"`.
2. An admin user opens the Agents tab and clicks the row's actions menu. Because `bot.role == "owner"`, the menu builds two role-change items: `Set role: No Access` and `Set role: Member`.
3. Admin clicks `Set role: No Access`. The handler fires:
   `POST /v1/agents/bot/role` with body `{"role":"no-access"}`.
4. Server hits the new last-owner guard (`agent.Role == "owner" && body.Role != "owner"`). `guardLastOwner` finds count == 1 and returns **409 "Cannot demote the last owner"**.
5. Client: `apiFetch` returns the 409 response. The onClick code does not check `resp.ok`. It calls `onDone()`, which calls `fetchData()`, which reloads the agent list. The dropdown closes.
6. The agent's role in the table is still `Owner` (because the server rejected the change). No banner, no toast, no console message. The admin has no idea why their click did nothing — they may even click the same item again, repeating the silent 409.

The same scenario on `AllUsersTab` correctly raises an error banner via `setError`, because the matching code path checks `resp.ok`.

## Fix

Mirror the `AllUsersTab` pattern: read `resp.ok`, parse the error JSON, surface via `setError`. `AllAgentsTab` already has a top-level `setError` available; the `RowActions` component just needs an `onError` prop wired through, exactly as `AllUsersTab` does. Roughly:

```tsx
items.push({
  label: `Set role: ${opt.label}`,
  onClick: async () => {
    const resp = await apiFetch(
      `/v1/agents/${encodeURIComponent(agent.name)}/role`,
      { method: "POST", body: JSON.stringify({ role: opt.role }) }
    );
    if (!resp.ok) {
      const data = await resp.json().catch(() => ({}));
      onError(data.error || "Failed to change role");
      return;
    }
    onDone();
  },
});
```

The silent-failure pattern was pre-existing for the toggle, but the PR enlarges the trigger surface and adds a fresh 409 path, and the asymmetry with the sibling `AllUsersTab` (also touched by this PR) makes the missed error surfacing glaring.

</details>

