body:
🔴 The new AbortController wiring in  has two cleanup gaps in the user-cancel path: (1) the `finally` block's `if (!controller.signal.aborted) setSubmitting(false)` guard skips the reset because `close()` flips `aborted=true` first, so `submitting` stays true forever and the Create button is permanently disabled (only a page refresh recovers); (2) the catch's early `return` on `AbortError` skips `onInvited()`, so if the server already persisted the create (or even redeemed it into a live agent session) before the abort fired, the list stays stale and the orphan/session is invisible until refresh. Both are regressions from d3274c1 — fix is one line each: drop the `!controller.signal.aborted` guard, and call `onInvited()` in the AbortError branch (or in `close()` before `abort()`).

<details>
<summary>Extended reasoning...</summary>

### Bug 1 — `submitting` flag wedges, Create button permanently disabled

**Code path (AllAgentsTab.tsx:351–397):**

```tsx
async function handleCreate() {
  if (!name.trim()) return;
  abortRef.current?.abort();
  const controller = new AbortController();
  abortRef.current = controller;
  setSubmitting(true);
  // ...
  try {
    /* two awaited fetches with signal: controller.signal */
  } catch (err) {
    if (err instanceof DOMException && err.name === "AbortError") return;
    setError("Network error.");
  } finally {
    if (!controller.signal.aborted) setSubmitting(false);  // <-- guard skips reset
  }
}
```

`close()` (line 323) calls `abortRef.current?.abort()` but never calls `setSubmitting(false)` itself. The aborted fetch rejects with `AbortError`; the catch returns; `finally` runs — but `controller.signal.aborted` is now `true` (we just aborted it), so `setSubmitting(false)` is **skipped**. `submitting` leaks at `true` for the lifetime of the `InviteAgentButton` component (the `Modal` returns null when closed, but `InviteAgentButton` itself stays mounted as part of `AllAgentsTab`, and no `useEffect` resets `submitting` on the `open` transition — grep confirms only three `setSubmitting` sites in the file).

**Impact:** Footer renders `<Button onClick={handleCreate} disabled={!name.trim()} loading={submitting}>Create invite</Button>`. `Button.tsx:28` sets `disabled={disabled || loading}` — so with `loading=true` the button is rendered disabled. Only a full page refresh recovers.

### Bug 2 — orphan invite / live agent session invisible after abort

The catch returns early on `AbortError` **without** calling `onInvited()`. But `abort()` is a client-side cancellation only — the server may have already received and committed the create POST (persisting a pending invite that reserves the agent name) and/or the redeem POST (which calls `CreateAgentToken` and returns `sess.ID`, producing a real, usable agent session — see `handle_agents.go:340–353`). The orphan/session then sits invisible in the agents list until the next page load. Worse, `handle_agents.go:1031` returns HTTP 409 "A pending invite for agent %q already exists" on retry, so the operator can't even re-create with the same name.

Notably, the author's own comment at lines 382–385 explicitly justifies calling `onInvited()` whenever the server may have persisted state ("a failed redeem leaves an orphan pending invite that reserves the agent name server-side, and the operator needs to see it in the list to revoke"). The AbortError branch is the prime case where that's most likely (the operator deliberately cancelled mid-flight) and is the one path that skips the refresh.

### Why existing safeguards don't catch this

The `!controller.signal.aborted` guard was clearly added to avoid clobbering a *fresh* `handleCreate` that overlaps the prior controller's abort (rapid-click race). But in the user-cancel-via-`close()` path no fresh `handleCreate` ever runs — the modal is closed — so the guard just wedges the UI. The race the guard protects against is already handled by the next `handleCreate` setting `submitting=true` again.

### Step-by-step proof (Bug 1)

1. Operator clicks "Create invite" — `handleCreate` runs: `setSubmitting(true)`, fires `POST /v1/agents/invites` (signal=controller.signal).
2. Network is slow (~300ms). Operator changes their mind, presses Escape → `close()` runs: `abortRef.current?.abort()`, `setOpen(false)`, `setName("")`, etc. **`submitting` is not touched.**
3. The aborted fetch rejects with `AbortError`. Catch matches `err.name === "AbortError"` → returns.
4. `finally` runs: `if (!controller.signal.aborted) setSubmitting(false)` — `signal.aborted` is `true`, so this is a no-op.
5. State after: `open=false`, `submitting=true`, all other fields reset.
6. Operator clicks "Add agent" again → `setOpen(true)`. `submitting` is still `true`.
7. Operator types "my-agent" → `Button.tsx:28` makes Create unclickable because `loading={submitting}` forces `disabled`. Only a full page refresh recovers.

### Step-by-step proof (Bug 2)

1. Operator clicks "Create invite" for "my-agent". `POST /v1/agents/invites` reaches the server, which persists the pending invite reserving the name. Response is mid-flight.
2. Operator presses Escape. `close()` aborts the controller. The pending fetch rejects with `AbortError`.
3. Catch returns without calling `onInvited()`. The agents list is stale.
4. Operator sees no pending row for "my-agent" and retries with the same name. Server returns 409 (`HasPendingInviteByAgentName` check at `handle_agents.go:1031`). The operator has no UI to revoke the orphan until the next page load.
5. Worse variant — if the abort fires after the create *and* redeem POSTs reached the server: `store.CreateAgentToken` returns `sess.ID`, a live agent session exists, the client discards the response. The operator never sees the token but the agent is on the box.

### Fix

```tsx
} catch (err) {
  if (err instanceof DOMException && err.name === "AbortError") {
    onInvited();  // server may have persisted state; let the operator see it
    return;
  }
  setError("Network error.");
} finally {
  setSubmitting(false);  // unconditional — the rapid-click race is already handled by the next call setting it to true again
}
```

Or equivalently, move both `setSubmitting(false)` and `onInvited()` into `close()` before the `abort()` call.

</details>

