body:
🔴 The four fixes the maintainer said landed in 9a89efb (replies to comments 3236261987 and 3236261997) are not in HEAD — commit 9a89efb is not in git history. The two user-reachable regressions remain: with `--mitm-port 0` `handleCreate` still mints a session before `InviteResultView` does the MITM probe, and the early-return then loses the token; and the inline `.catch(() => ({}))` on `redeemResp.json()` (line 381) still swallows AbortError, so an Escape during body-read leaves `inviteResult={agentToken: undefined}` and the next "Add agent" click shows `export AGENT_VAULT_TOKEN="undefined"`. The two defensive guards the maintainer described (`av_agent_token` presence check, `createData.token` presence check) are also missing. Re-apply all four — the claimed commit appears to have never been pushed.

<details>
<summary>Extended reasoning...</summary>

### Summary

The maintainer's replies on 2026-05-13 ([3236292245](https://github.com/Infisical/agent-vault/pull/169#discussion_r3236292245), [3236292387](https://github.com/Infisical/agent-vault/pull/169#discussion_r3236292387)) marked four issues as fixed in commit `9a89efb`. That commit is not in the branch. `git log` on HEAD `f952631` shows the most recent commits are `f952631, afbf347, 9d5aa9b, d3274c1, f47e8b8, 2ae19fc, 4535a94` — none touch the MITM probe, the inline body-parse catch, or either presence guard. The current state of `web/src/pages/home/AllAgentsTab.tsx` confirms all four regressions are still present:

```
$ git log --all --oneline | grep 9a89efb
(no output)
```

### 1. No upstream MITM probe (user-reachable, normal severity)

Lines 351–403 of `handleCreate`:

```tsx
setSubmitting(true);
setError("");
try {
  const payload: Record<string, unknown> = { name: name.trim() };
  // ...
  const createResp = await apiFetch("/v1/agents/invites", { ... });   // mints invite
  const createData = await createResp.json();
  // ...
  const redeemResp = await apiFetch(\`/invite/${createData.token}\`, { ... });  // mints session
```

No `/v1/mitm/ca.pem` probe before the redeem POST. The MITM check still lives inside `InviteResultView` at lines 604–617, with the early-return at 623–632 that renders **only** the "Transparent proxy is disabled" warning and skips the env snippet at 635–639 — the only place `agentToken` is rendered.

**Proof (`--mitm-port 0`):**
1. Server started with `--mitm-port 0` (`handle_mitm.go:21–27` returns 404 because `s.mitm` is nil).
2. Operator: Add agent → "my-agent" → Create invite.
3. `POST /v1/agents/invites` → 200, pending invite persisted.
4. `POST /invite/{token}` → 200, `store.CreateAgentToken` mints `sess.ID` (`handle_agents.go:340`). Session is live.
5. `setInviteResult({agentToken: \"sess_xyz\"})` → `InviteResultView` mounts.
6. HEAD `/v1/mitm/ca.pem` → 404. `mitm.available = false`.
7. Component returns only the warning panel. Env snippet at lines 635–639 never executes.
8. Operator clicks Done → `close()` → `setInviteResult(null)`. Token is irrecoverable.
9. Revoke + recreate with same name → step 2 repeats, next token lost too. Agent sessions are returned exactly once at redemption; the agents list API never re-exposes `av_agent_token`.

### 2. Inline `.catch(() => ({}))` swallows AbortError (user-reachable, normal severity)

Line 381:

```tsx
const redeemData = await redeemResp.json().catch(() => ({}));
```

The maintainer's reply 3236292387 stated this was changed to rethrow AbortError. The actual line at HEAD is the simple swallowing form. The PR diff shows the intended fix but the code on disk does not contain it (`isAbortError` is not even imported in this file, and a repo-wide grep returns zero hits for `isAbortError` in `web/src`).

**Proof (Escape during body read):**
1. `POST /invite/{token}` reaches server; `CreateAgentToken` mints `sess_xyz`; server begins streaming 200 response.
2. Headers arrive at client. Per WHATWG Fetch, `redeemResp.ok` is fixed at `true` once headers land — body-read aborts do not retroactively flip it.
3. Operator presses Escape → `close()` calls `abortRef.current?.abort()` then `setInviteResult(null)`.
4. Aborted controller cancels the body `ReadableStream`; `redeemResp.json()` rejects with `DOMException 'AbortError'`.
5. `.catch(() => ({}))` resolves the rejection to `{}`. Execution continues.
6. Line 385: `onInvited()` runs (list refreshes).
7. Line 386: `if (!redeemResp.ok)` is false — error branch skipped.
8. Line 390: `setInviteResult({ agentToken: undefined })` overwrites the `null` from step 3.
9. `finally`: `abortRef.current === controller` is true, so `setSubmitting(false)` runs.
10. State: `open=false`, `inviteResult={agentToken: undefined}`, `submitting=false`.
11. Next "Add agent" click flips `open=true`; `inviteResult` is the truthy `{agentToken: undefined}` object, so the modal jumps straight into `InviteResultView`.
12. Env snippet at line 637 template-literal-coerces `undefined` → `export AGENT_VAULT_TOKEN=\"undefined\"`. Operator copies the literal string into their shell.
13. The real `sess_xyz` is live server-side but irrecoverable from the UI.

### 3. Missing `av_agent_token` presence guard (nit, defense in depth)

Line 386–390:

```tsx
if (!redeemResp.ok) {
  setError(redeemData.message || redeemData.error || "Failed to issue agent token.");
  return;
}
setInviteResult({ agentToken: redeemData.av_agent_token });
```

No `if (!redeemData.av_agent_token)` guard. Maintainer reply 3236292387 explicitly claimed this guard landed. It didn't. Realistic trigger after fixing #2: schema drift in a future server version, intermediary cutting the body so JSON happens to parse but the field is missing, or any 200/empty-token edge case. Failure mode is the same literal `"undefined"` render.

### 4. Missing `createData.token` presence guard (nit, defense in depth)

Lines 371–376 jump straight from the create-response `!ok` check into the redeem POST. The PR diff in the metadata explicitly shows the guard the maintainer described, but it is not in HEAD. Practical impact is bounded today because `handle_agents.go:1115` deterministically writes `\"token\": inv.Token` on success — so this is a schema-drift / malformed-proxy guard. If triggered, the redeem POST hits `/invite/undefined` → 404 "Invite not found" → operator sees a misleading redeem-side error for a create-side anomaly.

### Fix

Re-apply the four changes the PR diff already describes:

```tsx
// upstream MITM probe at the top of handleCreate's try block
const mitmResp = await apiFetch("/v1/mitm/ca.pem", { method: "HEAD", signal: controller.signal });
if (mitmResp.status === 404) { setError("Transparent proxy is disabled on this server. …"); return; }
if (!mitmResp.ok) { setError(\`Couldn't reach the transparent proxy (HTTP ${mitmResp.status})…\`); return; }

// after createResp.json()
if (!createData.token) { setError("Server returned no invite token. Try again."); return; }

// inline catch on redeemResp.json()
const redeemData = await redeemResp.json().catch((err) => {
  if (isAbortError(err)) throw err;
  return {};
});

// after the !redeemResp.ok branch
if (!redeemData.av_agent_token) { setError("Server returned no agent token. Try again."); return; }
```

The first two close the user-reachable regressions; the latter two close the defensive gaps the maintainer explicitly committed to. They are independent — please don't skip the rethrow and the presence guards just because the MITM probe alone closes the loudest path.

</details>

